---
import DocsCodeBlock from "../../components/docs/DocsCodeBlock.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocsRelated from "../../components/docs/DocsRelated.astro";

/* Every way out goes through `ic uninstall` (_sandbox/ic/src/uninstall/), so the choices and rules below are its own. */

const unix = `curl -fsSL https://intentic.dev/uninstall | sh`;

const windows = `irm https://intentic.dev/uninstall | iex`;

const flagsUnix = `# See what would go, and change nothing.
curl -fsSL https://intentic.dev/uninstall | sh -s -- --dry-run

# With ic installed, the same command without the download.
ic uninstall --dry-run`;

const flagsWindows = `& ([scriptblock]::Create((irm https://intentic.dev/uninstall))) -DryRun`;

const unattended = `# Keep the sandboxes and saved passwords. Remove the app, folder sync and the caches.
ic uninstall --keep-sandboxes --yes

# Delete everything, data included. 3 is how many sandboxes and runners --dry-run counted.
ic uninstall --delete-data --confirm 3`;

const server = `curl -fsSL https://intentic.dev/cleanup-host | sudo sh`;

const oneSandbox = `curl -fsSL https://intentic.dev/remove | sh`;

const choices = [
    { what: `Sandboxes and runners, with their files`, everything: `Deleted`, keep: `Stay, and keep running` },
    { what: `Saved passwords, in the keyring on this computer`, everything: `Deleted`, keep: `Stay` },
    { what: `The Intentic engine (Windows)`, everything: `Removed`, keep: `Stays` },
    { what: `The machine agent, intentic-machine`, everything: `Removed`, keep: `Stays` },
    { what: `Folder sync`, everything: `Stopped and removed`, keep: `Stopped and removed` },
    { what: `ic, the command-line tool`, everything: `Removed`, keep: `Stays` },
    { what: `The desktop app`, everything: `Removed`, keep: `Removed` },
    { what: `Caches`, everything: `Deleted`, keep: `Deleted` },
    { what: `Intentic's other files and logs`, everything: `Deleted`, keep: `Stay` },
];
---

<DocsLayout
    pageId="uninstall"
    heading="Uninstall Intentic"
    lead="Take Intentic off a computer from Windows Settings, from the desktop app, or with one command in a terminal. Every way shows what it found first, and asks before it removes anything."
>
    <h2 id="start">Three ways to start</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Windows Settings.</strong> Open <strong>Apps</strong>, find <strong>Intentic</strong> in the list and choose{" "}
            <strong>Uninstall</strong>. Intentic's uninstaller opens on a page with the two choices below.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The desktop app.</strong> Open <strong>This device</strong> and choose <strong>Uninstall Intentic…</strong>. The same two
            choices, in the app's window.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A terminal, on any system.</strong> Run one command on the computer you want to clean up:
        </li>
    </ul>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={unix} lang="bash" title="Linux, macOS, WSL" />
    <DocsCodeBlock code={windows} lang="powershell" title="Windows (PowerShell)" />
    <p>
        The command uses the <code>ic</code> already on the computer, whether you installed it or the desktop app carries it. It downloads{" "}
        <code>ic</code> only when there is none, then runs <code>ic uninstall</code>, which does the rest. Where <code>ic</code> is
        installed, <code>ic uninstall</code> on its own is the same thing. Running it twice is safe: the second run finds what is left and
        removes only that.
    </p>
    <p>
        On a Windows PC, run it in PowerShell rather than inside a WSL distro. Removing everything from the Windows side also removes Intentic
        from the PC's WSL distros.
    </p>

    <h2 id="choices">The two choices</h2>
    <p>
        First it lists what it found: the sandboxes and runners and the space they use, saved passwords, the Intentic engine, the machine
        agent and the folders it syncs, the desktop app, and the caches. Then it asks one question with two answers, and the first one is
        already selected:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Remove everything.</strong> Nothing of Intentic stays on this computer.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Keep my sandboxes.</strong> The sandboxes stay and keep running, and so does what runs them. Only the desktop app,
            folder sync and the caches go.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>On this computer</th><th>Remove everything</th><th>Keep my sandboxes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                choices.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td>{row.what}</td>
                        <td>{row.everything}</td>
                        <td>{row.keep}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        Removing everything deletes the sandboxes for good, including any you removed in the last 7 days that could still have been
        restored. So when there are sandboxes, pressing Enter is not enough: the terminal asks you to type how many there are, and the
        Windows uninstaller and the app ask you to confirm. Until you do, nothing has been deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
        Keeping your sandboxes keeps everything they need to run: the engine they run on, the machine agent that starts them when you sign in
        and keeps them connected, <code>ic</code>, and the keyring their agents use. To take the rest off later, run the uninstall again
        and choose Remove everything.
    </p>
    <p>
        If you also run your own containers on the Intentic engine (connected to a WSL distro with <code>ic engine wsl</code>), the engine
        stays even when you remove everything, and the summary says how to remove it yourself.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stays">What always stays</h2>
    <p>
        These are either yours or shared with other programs on the computer, and an uninstall cannot tell what else depends on them. The
        summary at the end lists the ones this computer has.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Docker Desktop.</strong> Other containers and tools may use it. If nothing else needs it, uninstall it from Windows
            Settings like any other program.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>WSL and its Windows features.</strong> Other Linux distros may live there, and turning the features off needs a restart.
            Intentic's own distro, the engine, is removed with everything else.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your membership of the <code>docker</code> group</strong> on Linux. Your own <code>docker</code> commands may rely on it.{" "}
            <code>sudo gpasswd -d $USER docker</code> removes it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>The files in the folders you synced.</strong> Uninstalling stops the syncing and leaves the files where they are. After
            Remove everything, the copy on this computer is the only one left.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong><code>~/intentic/local</code>.</strong> The folder the desktop app opens when you have not picked one. What you saved
            there is yours.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        An uninstall only touches the computer it runs on. Sandboxes Intentic hosts for you, your account, and servers you connected for
        deployments are left as they are.
    </p>

    <h2 id="flags">Flags</h2>
    <p>
        Add them to <code>ic uninstall</code>, or after the one-liner. In PowerShell, <code>| iex</code> cannot pass flags, so run the
        script as a scriptblock and spell them <code>-DryRun</code>, <code>-KeepSandboxes</code>, <code>-Yes</code>,{" "}
        <code>-DeleteData</code> and <code>-Confirm &lt;n&gt;</code>:
    </p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={flagsUnix} lang="bash" title="Linux, macOS, WSL" />
    <DocsCodeBlock code={flagsWindows} lang="powershell" title="Windows (PowerShell)" />
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Flag</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><code>--dry-run</code></td>
                <td>
                    Lists what it found and what each choice would remove, and removes nothing. It also says the number{" "}
                    <code>--confirm</code> needs. With <code>--json</code>, the same as one JSON document for a program to read.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>--keep-sandboxes</code></td>
                <td>Picks Keep my sandboxes without the question. On a terminal it still asks yes or no, unless you add <code>--yes</code>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>--yes</code></td>
                <td>
                    Answers the question without asking it. With sandboxes or saved passwords on the computer it keeps them, as Keep my
                    sandboxes does. With neither, it removes everything.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><code>--delete-data --confirm &lt;n&gt;</code></td>
                <td>
                    Picks Remove everything, sandboxes and saved passwords included. <code>&lt;n&gt;</code> is the number of sandboxes and
                    runners on the computer, counting removed ones that could still be restored. A different number stops the run before
                    anything changes. With saved passwords and no sandboxes, <code>--delete-data --yes</code> does the same.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 id="unattended">Unattended</h2>
    <p>
        A run nobody answers never deletes a sandbox or a saved password unless you name the deletion. That covers <code>--yes</code>, a
        terminal-less run, and a silent uninstall from Windows (the uninstaller's <code>/S</code>, as software-management tools run it).
        Each of these keeps the data and does what Keep my sandboxes does. To delete it unattended, say so with{" "}
        <code>--delete-data</code>, and give the count:
    </p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={unattended} lang="bash" title="Scripts and management tools" />
    <p>
        A run with no terminal and none of these flags stops before it changes anything, and names the flag that would answer. It ends
        with <strong>0</strong> when it finished or you said no, <strong>1</strong> when a step failed or nobody was there to answer (the
        last lines say which step and what to do), and <strong>2</strong> when the command line itself was wrong.
    </p>

    <h2 id="servers">One sandbox, or a server</h2>
    <p>
        To take a single sandbox off a computer and keep Intentic, use <code>remove</code>. It lists the sandboxes and removes the one you
        pick, and <code>ic sandbox restore &lt;name&gt;</code> brings it back within 7 days:
    </p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={oneSandbox} lang="bash" title="One sandbox" />
    <p>
        A server you connected for deployments is not touched by uninstalling Intentic from your own computer. On the server, run:
    </p>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={server} lang="bash" title="On the server, as root" />
    <p>
        It runs <code>ic machine remove</code>, which lists what Intentic put on the server and asks once. Then it removes the deployed
        services and their volumes (databases included), <code>/opt/intentic</code>, the SSH tunnel connector and the service user. It
        keeps Docker Engine, the SSH server and the <code>cloudflared</code> program. It cannot reach your Cloudflare account, so remove the
        server on the <strong>Infrastructure</strong> page too and apply: that deletes its tunnels and DNS records.
    </p>

    <DocsRelated
        links={[
            { href: "/docs/your-machine/", title: "Your own machine", why: "what the machine agent, folder sync and a runner do while they are installed." },
            { href: "/docs/docker/", title: "Docker setup", why: "the containers and volumes a sandbox is made of, which Remove everything deletes." },
            { href: "/docs/troubleshooting/", title: "Troubleshooting", why: "when the problem is a sandbox that stopped answering, not Intentic itself." },
        ]}
    />
</DocsLayout>
